#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# min

#% text
# `min(a, b, ...)` gives the smallest of the real numbers `a`, `b`, ....

#% text
# `min(xs)` gives the smallest element of the list `xs`; nested lists are searched through.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Numbers of every kind are compared by value; closed numeric expressions such as `e` are not (the call stays).

#% item
# With symbolic arguments the call stays, keeping only the arguments that may still be the smallest.

#% item
# `min([])` is a failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
min(3, 7, 2)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
min([4, 1, 9, 2])
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
min([[1, 5], [3, -2]])
#% output
#= -2

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
min(1/3, 0.3, 2/7)
#% output
#= 2/7

#% cell
min(x, 2, 5)
#% output
#= min(x, 2, 5)

#% cell
min([])
#% output
#! failure(argument, min, 1)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# An argument cut at the end of a table's range, as the wall-profile script does before calling an interpolation:

#% cell
fn capped(x) = min(x, 25.0)
[capped(3.0), capped(40.0)]
#% output
#= [3.0, 25.0]

#% text
# The nearest point to a target:

#% cell
pts = [1.5, 4.2, 2.9]
min(abs(pts - 3))
#% output
#= 0.10000000000000009

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `min` of a list is the first element of the sorted list:

#% cell
v = [5, 3, 8]
min(v) == first(sort(v))
#% output
#= true

#% text
# `min` and `max` together clamp a value:

#% cell
min(max(-4, 0), 10)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Complex numbers have no order:

#% cell
min(1, 2im)
#% output
#= min(1, 2im)

#% text
# A closed numeric expression is not compared until it is a number:

#% cell
min(e, 2.7, 11/4)
#% output
#= min(e, 2.7, 11/4)

#% cell
min(n(e), 2.7, 11/4)
#% output
#= 2.7

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `max`, `min_by`, `clamp`, `sort`, `find_minimum`
